# Lares — example environment configuration
#
# Copy to `.env` (git-ignored) only if you need to override a default below.
# Lares runs WITHOUT any of these set — every value has a working default.
#
# IMPORTANT: Lares needs NO secrets in this file to run. Your AI provider
# credentials live in the terminal-agent CLI you use (e.g. Claude Code's own
# login/config), NOT here. Keep long-lived secrets out of workspaces you open
# in Lares — see SECURITY.md.
#
# The app sets its own internal variables (AGENT_ID, AGENT_DASHBOARD_API_TOKEN,
# TEAM_ID, and the per-agent AGENT_DASHBOARD_* handshake vars) automatically
# when it launches agents. Do not set those here.

# --- Local HTTP API (the dashboard's own control-plane server) -----------------
# Host/port the in-app API server binds to. Change only if the default port
# collides with something else on your machine.
# DASHBOARD_HOST=127.0.0.1
# DASHBOARD_PORT=4317

# --- Jupyter (optional — only if you point Lares at an external notebook server)-
# Leave unset to use the notebook server Lares manages for you.
# JUPYTER_BASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8888
# How to get JUPYTER_TOKEN: it is printed by your external Jupyter server on
# startup (or run `jupyter server list` to see the token in the server URL).
# Set it in a separate terminal / your own editor — never paste secrets into an
# AI session. Only needed if JUPYTER_BASE_URL points at a server you run yourself.
# JUPYTER_TOKEN=

# --- Codex provider (optional — only if you use Codex for cross-provider work) --
# Location of your Codex CLI home/config directory.
# CODEX_HOME=

# --- Advanced / development knobs ---------------------------------------------
# Standard Node environment flag.
# NODE_ENV=development
# Restrict which MCP toolsets the dashboard MCP server exposes (comma-separated).
# DASHBOARD_MCP_TOOLSETS=
